        "id": "c1-l2",
        "title": "Lesson 2: Managing Debt",
        "introduction": "Debt is one of the most misunderstood forces in personal finance. Used wisely, it can fund \neducation that multiplies earning power, purchase a home that builds equity over decades, or \nsmooth over a short-term cash flow gap. Used poorly, it becomes a slow drain on your finances - \ninterest payments that consume income month after month, leaving less for everything else. \n \nThe key difference between productive and destructive debt is the interest rate and the purpose. \nA mortgage at 6.5% on a home that appreciates in value is very different from a credit card at \n24% APR on discretionary purchases. Student loans may be worth taking on for degrees with \nstrong earning potential; they become burdensome when taken on for programs with weak job \nmarket outcomes. \n \nUnderstanding how interest works - particularly compound interest working against you - is \nessential. A $5,000 credit card balance at 24% APR, paying only the $100 minimum payment \neach month, will take over 20 years to pay off and cost more than $8,000 in interest. The debt \ndoubles before it is eliminated. Millions of Americans get stuck in this cycle. \n \nThis lesson explains the types of debt, the strategies to eliminate it efficiently, how to protect your \ncredit score during the process, and how to avoid the patterns that lead people back into debt \nafter paying it off.",
