        "lessonContent": "Term <br/>Definition <br/>Interest Rate (APR) <br/>Annual Percentage Rate - the yearly cost of borrowing money <br/>expressed as a percentage, including fees. <br/>Debt Avalanche <br/>A payoff strategy where you pay minimums on all debts, then apply all <br/>extra money to the highest-interest debt first, minimizing total interest <br/>paid. <br/>Debt Snowball <br/>A payoff strategy where you pay minimums on all debts, then <br/>aggressively pay off the smallest balance first, building psychological <br/>momentum. <br/>Credit Utilization <br/>The percentage of your total available credit you are currently using. <br/>Keeping this below 30% supports a strong credit score. <br/>Minimum Payment <br/>The smallest required payment each billing cycle to keep an account <br/>in good standing - usually 1-2% of the balance, barely covering <br/>interest. <br/>Debt-to-Income Ratio <br/>(DTI) <br/>Monthly debt payments divided by gross monthly income. Lenders use <br/>this to evaluate creditworthiness; below 36% is generally considered <br/>healthy. <br/> <br/>Personal Finance Foundations  |  Page 7 <br/>Term <br/>Definition <br/>Debt Consolidation <br/>Combining multiple debts into a single loan, ideally at a lower interest <br/>rate, to simplify payments and reduce total interest. <br/>Charge-Off <br/>When a lender writes off a debt as uncollectible after non-payment, <br/>typically after 180 days. Severely damages credit and may lead to <br/>collections. <br/>Understanding How Interest Works Against You <br/>Interest is the price you pay to borrow money. When you carry a balance on a credit card, interest <br/>accrues daily on the outstanding balance. At 24% APR, the daily rate is approximately 0.066%. <br/>A $3,000 balance accumulates about $2 of interest every day - $60 per month - just to stay in <br/>place. <br/> <br/>That's why minimum payments are so dangerous. When the minimum payment on a $3,000 <br/>balance is $60, and $60 of that is interest, your principal barely decreases. You can pay faithfully <br/>for years and make almost no progress. Credit card companies are legally required to show how <br/>long it will take to pay off a balance paying only the minimum - look for that disclosure on your <br/>statement. <br/> <br/>The fix is paying more than the minimum. Every extra dollar applied to principal reduces future <br/>interest charges. Even an additional $50 per month can cut years off a repayment timeline and <br/>save hundreds or thousands in interest. <br/>Debt Payoff Strategies: Avalanche vs. Snowball <br/>The two most popular debt payoff strategies are the Debt Avalanche and the Debt Snowball. The <br/>difference is in which debt you pay first, and each has its own strengths. <br/> <br/>The Debt Avalanche is the mathematically optimal strategy. List all debts by interest rate, highest <br/>to lowest. Pay the minimum on every debt except the highest-rate one, which receives every extra <br/>dollar available. Once that debt is eliminated, roll its payment to the next highest rate. This method <br/>minimizes the total interest paid over the life of all debts. <br/> <br/>The Debt Snowball, popularized by financial educator Dave Ramsey, targets the smallest balance <br/>first regardless of interest rate. The mathematical cost is slightly higher, but the psychological <br/>benefit is significant - early wins build momentum and motivation that helps people stay <br/>committed. Research shows that many people who start with the Avalanche give up before seeing <br/>results; the Snowball keeps them engaged. <br/> <br/>The best strategy is the one you will stick with. If motivation is your challenge, start with the <br/>Snowball. If you are disciplined and want to minimize total cost, use the Avalanche. You can also <br/>create a hybrid: knock out one or two small balances first for momentum, then switch to the <br/>Avalanche for the larger debts. <br/> <br/>Personal Finance Foundations  |  Page 8 <br/>Protecting and Rebuilding Your Credit Score <br/>Your credit score affects more than just loan approvals. Landlords check credit scores when <br/>screening tenants. Employers in certain industries review credit as part of background checks. <br/>Insurance companies in many states use credit-based insurance scores to determine premiums. <br/>A strong credit score saves thousands over a lifetime; a poor one costs thousands. <br/> <br/>Five things make up your FICO score: payment history (35%), amounts owed/credit utilization <br/>(30%), length of credit history (15%), credit mix (10%), and new credit inquiries (10%). Paying on <br/>time, every time, and keeping utilization below 30% address 65% of your score. <br/> <br/>Common mistakes that hurt your score: closing old credit cards after paying them off (this reduces <br/>available credit and shortens credit history), applying for multiple new accounts in a short period <br/>(each application generates a hard inquiry), and co-signing loans for others (you're equally liable <br/>for their payments or defaults).<br/><br/>### Real-World Example<br/>James has three debts: a $500 medical bill at 0% interest, a $3,000 credit card at 24% APR, and <br/>a $10,000 personal loan at 10% APR. He has $300 per month to put toward extra payments. Using <br/>the Debt Avalanche, he targets the credit card first. At $300 extra per month plus minimums, he <br/>eliminates the credit card in about 10 months, saving over $600 in interest compared to minimum-<br/>only payments. He then rolls that payment to the personal loan. Using the Snowball, James would <br/>knock out the $500 medical bill in less than two months first, getting an early win before attacking <br/>the larger balances. Both strategies are valid - the choice depends on whether James is motivated <br/>more by math or momentum.<br/><br/>### Quiz Questions<br/># <br/>Question <br/>Answer <br/>1 <br/>What is APR? <br/>Annual Percentage Rate - the yearly cost of <br/>borrowing money expressed as a percentage. <br/>2 <br/>How does the Debt Avalanche differ from <br/>the Debt Snowball? <br/>Avalanche targets highest-interest debt first (saves <br/>the most money); Snowball targets smallest balance <br/>first (provides more psychological motivation). <br/>3 <br/>What credit utilization rate is recommended <br/>for a good credit score? <br/>Below 30% of your total available credit limit. <br/>4 <br/>What is debt consolidation? <br/>Combining multiple debts into one loan, typically <br/>with a lower interest rate to reduce total interest <br/>paid. <br/>5 <br/>What is a debt-to-income ratio? <br/>Monthly debt payments divided by gross monthly <br/>income, used by lenders to assess repayment <br/>capacity and risk. <br/>6 <br/>Why are minimum payments dangerous on <br/>credit cards? <br/>Because they barely cover interest charges, leaving <br/>principal nearly unchanged - extending repayment <br/>for decades at significant cost. <br/>7 <br/>What is a charge-off? <br/>When a lender writes off a debt as uncollectible <br/>after non-payment, severely damaging the <br/>borrower's credit score. <br/>8 <br/>Why is closing paid-off credit cards often a <br/>mistake? <br/>It reduces available credit and can shorten credit <br/>history length, both of which can lower your credit <br/>score. <br/> <br/> <br/> <br/>Personal Finance Foundations  |  Page 10",
