        "id": "c2-l5",
        "title": "Lesson 5: Exploring Stocks and Sectors",
        "introduction": "",
        "flashcards": [
          {
            "term": "Note",
            "definition": "Key terms are listed in the lesson content below."
          }
        ],
        "lessonContent": "Term <br/>Definition <br/>Sector <br/>A group of companies operating in the same industry (e.g., <br/>Technology, Healthcare, Energy). <br/>Cyclical Stock <br/>A stock whose performance closely follows the economic cycle - <br/>rising in expansions, falling in recessions. <br/>Defensive Stock <br/>A stock that tends to be stable regardless of the economic cycle <br/>(e.g., utilities, consumer staples). <br/>Market Capitalization <br/>A company's total stock market value: share price multiplied by <br/>total shares outstanding. <br/>Large Cap <br/>Companies with market caps over $10 billion - typically more <br/>stable, established businesses. <br/>Small Cap <br/>Companies with market caps of $300 million to $2 billion - higher <br/>growth potential and higher risk. <br/>Sector Rotation <br/>Shifting portfolio allocation between sectors as the economic cycle <br/>changes. <br/>GICS <br/>Global Industry Classification Standard - the system used to <br/>classify stocks into 11 sectors. <br/>Growth Stock <br/>A company that reinvests earnings aggressively to expand; <br/>typically trades at higher valuations. <br/>Value Stock <br/>A company trading below its perceived intrinsic value, often with <br/>steady earnings and dividends. <br/> <br/>Personal Finance Foundations  |  Page 62 <br/>Term <br/>Definition <br/>Sector ETF <br/>An exchange-traded fund providing diversified exposure to all <br/>companies within a specific sector. <br/>Concentration Risk <br/>The risk of having too large a portion of your portfolio in a single <br/>sector or company. <br/> <br/>The Economic Cycle and Sector Rotation <br/>The economy moves through four broad phases: expansion, peak, contraction (recession), and <br/>recovery. Savvy investors adjust sector allocations as the cycle progresses, rotating capital <br/>toward sectors positioned to outperform in the current phase. <br/>\u2022 <br/>Expansion: Technology, Consumer Discretionary, and Financials tend to lead as <br/>consumers and businesses spend freely. <br/>\u2022 <br/>Peak: Energy and Materials often outperform as commodity demand peaks along with <br/>economic activity. <br/>\u2022 <br/>Contraction (Recession): Defensive sectors - Healthcare, Utilities, Consumer Staples - <br/>hold up best as spending shifts to essentials. <br/>\u2022 <br/>Recovery: Industrials and Financials often lead coming out of recessions as credit and <br/>capital spending resume. <br/>In practice, perfectly timing sector rotation is extremely difficult. Most investors are better served <br/>by maintaining broad sector diversification and using sector tilts (overweighting or <br/>underweighting) rather than wholesale rotation.<br/><br/>### Real-World Example<br/>Case Study: The 2022 Sector Divergence <br/>In 2022, rising interest rates triggered a dramatic sector divergence. The Information <br/>Technology sector fell approximately 33% as high valuations deflated under higher discount <br/>rates. Meanwhile, the Energy sector surged approximately 65% as oil prices spiked following <br/>geopolitical events. Investors who held energy exposure (even a modest 5-10% allocation) <br/>significantly outperformed those concentrated in technology. This dramatic divergence illustrated <br/>why sector diversification is not optional - no sector outperforms forever.<br/><br/>### Quiz Questions<br/># <br/>Question <br/>Answer <br/>1 <br/>What is the difference between a <br/>cyclical and defensive stock? <br/>Cyclical stocks follow economic cycles; <br/>defensive stocks (utilities, staples) remain stable <br/>through cycles. <br/>2 <br/>What is sector rotation? <br/>Shifting portfolio allocations between sectors as <br/>the economic cycle changes phases. <br/>3 <br/>What is market capitalization? <br/>A company's share price multiplied by total <br/>shares outstanding - its total stock market value. <br/>4 <br/>What are the 11 GICS sectors? <br/>Energy, Materials, Industrials, Utilities, <br/>Healthcare, Financials, Consumer Discretionary, <br/>Consumer Staples, IT, Communication Services, <br/>Real Estate. <br/>5 <br/>What is the difference between growth <br/>and value stocks? <br/>Growth stocks prioritize expansion and trade at <br/>higher valuations; value stocks trade below their <br/>perceived intrinsic value. <br/>6 <br/>Which sectors tend to outperform during <br/>a recession? <br/>Defensive sectors - Healthcare, Utilities, and <br/>Consumer Staples - as spending shifts to <br/>essentials. <br/>7 <br/>What is a sector ETF? <br/>An exchange-traded fund providing diversified <br/>exposure to all companies within a single sector. <br/> <br/> <br/> <br/>Personal Finance Foundations  |  Page 64",
        "realWorldExamples": "Case Study: The 2022 Sector Divergence \nIn 2022, rising interest rates triggered a dramatic sector divergence. The Information \nTechnology sector fell approximately 33% as high valuations deflated under higher discount \nrates. Meanwhile, the Energy sector surged approximately 65% as oil prices spiked following \ngeopolitical events. Investors who held energy exposure (even a modest 5-10% allocation) \nsignificantly outperformed those concentrated in technology. This dramatic divergence illustrated \nwhy sector diversification is not optional - no sector outperforms forever.",
