        "id": "c2-l6",
        "title": "Lesson 6: Investing for Income",
        "introduction": "",
        "flashcards": [
          {
            "term": "Note",
            "definition": "Key terms are listed in the lesson content below."
          }
        ],
        "lessonContent": "Term <br/>Definition <br/>Dividend <br/>A portion of a company's earnings paid to shareholders, typically <br/>quarterly in cash. <br/>Dividend Yield <br/>Annual dividends per share divided by the stock price - shows <br/>income relative to investment. <br/>Dividend Aristocrat <br/>An S&P 500 company that has increased its dividend every year <br/>for at least 25 consecutive years. <br/>Ex-Dividend Date <br/>The cutoff date by which you must own a stock to receive its next <br/>scheduled dividend payment. <br/>DRIP <br/>Dividend Reinvestment Plan - automatically reinvests dividends to <br/>purchase additional shares. <br/>Payout Ratio <br/>The percentage of earnings a company pays as dividends; ratios <br/>above 75-80% may be unsustainable. <br/>Preferred Stock <br/>A hybrid security paying fixed dividends with priority over common <br/>stock in liquidation. <br/>Yield Trap <br/>An unusually high dividend yield signaling financial distress - the <br/>dividend may soon be cut. <br/>Coverage Ratio <br/>How many times a company can cover its dividend from earnings <br/>or cash flow - higher is safer. <br/>Bond Coupon <br/>The fixed interest payment paid by a bond to its holder, typically <br/>semi-annually. <br/> <br/>Personal Finance Foundations  |  Page 65 <br/>Term <br/>Definition <br/>Dividend Growth Rate <br/>The annual rate at which a company increases its dividend - key <br/>for long-term income projections. <br/> <br/>Dividend Investing Deep Dive <br/>Understanding Dividend Yield <br/>A dividend yield of 3-4% on a stock represents the income you receive per dollar invested. But <br/>yield alone tells only part of the story. A yield of 8% might look attractive until you realize it's that <br/>high because the stock price has crashed - often a warning sign the dividend is about to be cut. <br/>The Power of Dividend Growth <br/>Investors often make the mistake of choosing the highest current yield rather than the best <br/>dividend growth profile. Consider: a stock yielding 2% that grows its dividend 10% per year will <br/>yield 5.2% on your original investment after 10 years and 13.5% after 20 years - significantly <br/>outperforming a static 5% yielder over time. <br/>Dividend Aristocrats <br/>Companies that have increased dividends every year for 25+ consecutive years demonstrate <br/>remarkable financial resilience. They've maintained and grown dividends through recessions, <br/>wars, financial crises, and pandemics. Examples include Procter & Gamble (67+ years of <br/>consecutive increases), Johnson & Johnson, and Coca-Cola. These companies typically have <br/>durable competitive advantages, strong free cash flow, and disciplined capital allocation.<br/><br/>### Real-World Example<br/>Case Study: Helen's Dividend Income Strategy <br/>Helen builds a $500,000 income portfolio averaging a 4% dividend yield, generating <br/>$20,000/year in dividends ($1,667/month). During her working years, she enrolls in a DRIP - <br/>automatically reinvesting every dividend to buy additional shares. After 15 years, her $500,000 <br/>portfolio has grown to approximately $900,000 (at 4% growth + 4% reinvested yield). She then <br/>turns off the DRIP, begins collecting $36,000/year in dividends, and effectively has a self-funded <br/>'paycheck' in retirement that continues to grow each year as companies raise dividends.<br/><br/>### Quiz Questions<br/># <br/>Question <br/>Answer <br/>1 <br/>What is a dividend yield? <br/>Annual dividends per share divided by the stock <br/>price - shows income relative to the investment <br/>cost. <br/>2 <br/>What is a Dividend Aristocrat? <br/>An S&P 500 company that has raised its <br/>dividend for at least 25 consecutive years. <br/>3 <br/>What is a yield trap? <br/>A very high dividend yield signaling financial <br/>distress - the dividend may soon be cut or <br/>eliminated. <br/>4 <br/>What is a DRIP? <br/>A Dividend Reinvestment Plan that automatically <br/>reinvests dividends to purchase more shares. <br/>5 <br/>What payout ratio indicates a <br/>sustainable dividend? <br/>Generally below 60% - companies paying more <br/>may struggle to maintain dividends during <br/>economic downturns. <br/>6 <br/>Why is dividend growth rate important? <br/>A low yield growing fast will surpass a static high <br/>yield over time and outpace inflation. <br/>7 <br/>What is the ex-dividend date? <br/>The cutoff date by which you must own a stock <br/>to receive its next dividend payment. <br/> <br/> <br/> <br/>Personal Finance Foundations  |  Page 67",
        "realWorldExamples": "Case Study: Helen's Dividend Income Strategy \nHelen builds a $500,000 income portfolio averaging a 4% dividend yield, generating \n$20,000/year in dividends ($1,667/month). During her working years, she enrolls in a DRIP - \nautomatically reinvesting every dividend to buy additional shares. After 15 years, her $500,000 \nportfolio has grown to approximately $900,000 (at 4% growth + 4% reinvested yield). She then \nturns off the DRIP, begins collecting $36,000/year in dividends, and effectively has a self-funded \n'paycheck' in retirement that continues to grow each year as companies raise dividends.",
