        "id": "c2-l7",
        "title": "Lesson 7: Analyzing Stock Fundamentals",
        "introduction": "",
        "flashcards": [
          {
            "term": "Note",
            "definition": "Key terms are listed in the lesson content below."
          }
        ],
        "lessonContent": "Term <br/>Definition <br/>P/E Ratio <br/>Stock price divided by earnings per share - shows how much <br/>investors pay per dollar of earnings. <br/>EPS (Earnings Per <br/>Share) <br/>Net income divided by shares outstanding - a measure of <br/>profitability per share. <br/>Revenue <br/>Total income generated by a company from its business activities <br/>before deducting expenses. <br/>Free Cash Flow (FCF) <br/>Cash from operations minus capital expenditures - the real cash a <br/>business generates. <br/>Debt-to-Equity Ratio <br/>Total debt divided by shareholder equity - measures how much a <br/>company relies on borrowed money. <br/>Return on Equity (ROE) <br/>Net income divided by shareholder equity - measures <br/>management's efficiency generating profit. <br/>Intrinsic Value <br/>The estimated true worth of a company based on fundamentals, <br/>independent of market price. <br/>Margin of Safety <br/>Buying at a significant discount to intrinsic value to protect against <br/>errors in your analysis. <br/>Gross Margin <br/>Revenue minus cost of goods sold, divided by revenue - shows <br/>basic profitability of the core business. <br/> <br/>Personal Finance Foundations  |  Page 68 <br/>Term <br/>Definition <br/>Operating Margin <br/>Operating income divided by revenue - shows profitability after <br/>operating expenses but before interest and taxes. <br/>Price-to-Book (P/B) <br/>Ratio <br/>Stock price divided by book value per share - useful for valuing <br/>asset-heavy businesses. <br/>10-K Filing <br/>A company's comprehensive annual report filed with the SEC - the <br/>most important document for fundamental analysis. <br/> <br/>Key Ratios Explained <br/>Price-to-Earnings (P/E) Ratio <br/>The P/E ratio is the most widely used valuation metric. If a stock trades at $60 with earnings of <br/>$4/share, its P/E is 15 - investors are paying $15 for every $1 of annual earnings. A high P/E <br/>means the market expects strong future growth. A low P/E may indicate undervaluation - or <br/>problems. Always compare P/E within the same industry; tech companies naturally trade at <br/>higher P/Es than utility companies. <br/>Free Cash Flow <br/>Free cash flow is arguably the most important metric because it's the hardest to manipulate. <br/>Reported earnings can be influenced by accounting choices (depreciation methods, revenue <br/>recognition timing), but cash flow from operations tells you how much cash the business actually <br/>generated. Companies with high and growing free cash flow can pay dividends, buy back stock, <br/>reduce debt, or reinvest - all shareholder-friendly activities. <br/>Return on Equity (ROE) <br/>ROE measures how efficiently management generates profit from shareholders' money. A <br/>consistent ROE above 15-20% over many years suggests a durable competitive advantage (or <br/>'moat'). Warren Buffett has often cited high and consistent ROE as one of the hallmarks of a <br/>business worth owning at a fair price.<br/><br/>### Real-World Example<br/>Case Study: Laura's Comparative Analysis <br/>Laura analyzes two companies in the same sector. Company A has a P/E of 15, strong and <br/>growing free cash flow, 22% ROE, a 35% debt-to-equity ratio, and a 45% operating margin. <br/>Company B has a P/E of 80, negative free cash flow, 8% ROE, a 180% debt-to-equity ratio, and <br/>a 5% operating margin. Company B has a compelling product narrative and a popular stock - <br/>but the fundamentals are significantly weaker. Laura buys Company A, estimating an intrinsic <br/>value of $50/share against a current price of $40 - a 20% margin of safety that provides <br/>downside protection if her analysis is imperfect. <br/> <br/> <br/>Personal Finance Foundations  |  Page 69<br/><br/>### Quiz Questions<br/># <br/>Question <br/>Answer <br/>1 <br/>What does a P/E ratio tell you? <br/>How much investors pay per dollar of earnings - <br/>higher P/E means expectations of stronger future <br/>growth. <br/>2 <br/>What is free cash flow? <br/>Cash generated from operations minus capital <br/>expenditures - the real cash a business actually <br/>produces. <br/>3 <br/>What is the margin of safety? <br/>Buying at a significant discount to intrinsic value <br/>to protect against errors in analysis. <br/>4 <br/>What is Return on Equity (ROE)? <br/>Net income divided by shareholder equity - <br/>measures how efficiently management <br/>generates profit. <br/>5 <br/>Why is free cash flow considered more <br/>reliable than reported earnings? <br/>It's harder to manipulate through accounting <br/>choices and directly reflects actual cash <br/>generation. <br/>6 <br/>What is a company's intrinsic value? <br/>The estimated true worth based on its <br/>fundamentals, independent of the current market <br/>price. <br/>7 <br/>What is a 10-K filing? <br/>A company's comprehensive annual report filed <br/>with the SEC - the most important document for <br/>fundamental analysis. <br/> <br/>Personal Finance Foundations  |  Page 70 <br/> <br/> <br/> <br/>Personal Finance Foundations  |  Page 71",
        "realWorldExamples": "Case Study: Laura's Comparative Analysis \nLaura analyzes two companies in the same sector. Company A has a P/E of 15, strong and \ngrowing free cash flow, 22% ROE, a 35% debt-to-equity ratio, and a 45% operating margin. \nCompany B has a P/E of 80, negative free cash flow, 8% ROE, a 180% debt-to-equity ratio, and \na 5% operating margin. Company B has a compelling product narrative and a popular stock - \nbut the fundamentals are significantly weaker. Laura buys Company A, estimating an intrinsic \nvalue of $50/share against a current price of $40 - a 20% margin of safety that provides \ndownside protection if her analysis is imperfect. \n \n \nPersonal Finance Foundations  |  Page 69",
