        "id": "c2-l8",
        "title": "Lesson 8: Using Technical Analysis",
        "introduction": "",
        "flashcards": [
          {
            "term": "Note",
            "definition": "Key terms are listed in the lesson content below."
          }
        ],
        "lessonContent": "Term <br/>Definition <br/>Support Level <br/>A price level where a stock historically stops falling and buyers <br/>tend to step in - a price 'floor.' <br/>Resistance Level <br/>A price level where a stock historically stops rising and sellers <br/>dominate - a price 'ceiling.' <br/>Moving Average (MA) <br/>The average price of a stock over a specified period, smoothing out <br/>short-term fluctuations. <br/>RSI (Relative Strength <br/>Index) <br/>A momentum indicator (0-100); above 70 = potentially overbought, <br/>below 30 = potentially oversold. <br/>MACD <br/>Moving Average Convergence Divergence - a trend-following <br/>momentum indicator using two moving averages. <br/>Breakout <br/>When a stock price moves above resistance or below support, <br/>often accompanied by high volume. <br/>Candlestick Chart <br/>A price chart showing open, high, low, and closing prices for each <br/>period using colored bars. <br/>Volume Confirmation <br/>A price move accompanied by high trading volume, increasing the <br/>reliability of the signal. <br/>Death Cross <br/>When the 50-day moving average crosses below the 200-day <br/>moving average - a bearish signal. <br/> <br/>Personal Finance Foundations  |  Page 72 <br/>Term <br/>Definition <br/>Golden Cross <br/>When the 50-day moving average crosses above the 200-day <br/>moving average - a bullish signal. <br/>Trend Line <br/>A line drawn connecting successive lows (uptrend) or highs <br/>(downtrend) to visualize direction. <br/>Divergence <br/>When price makes new highs/lows but an indicator (like RSI) does <br/>not - can signal trend reversal. <br/> <br/>Core Technical Concepts <br/>Support and Resistance <br/>Support and resistance levels are the foundation of chart analysis. Support is a price level <br/>where buying interest is strong enough to prevent the price from falling further. Resistance is a <br/>level where selling pressure is strong enough to prevent the price from rising further. When a <br/>support level is broken, it often becomes a new resistance level - and vice versa. These 'role <br/>reversals' are highly reliable trading signals. <br/>Moving Averages <br/>The 50-day and 200-day moving averages are the most widely watched technical indicators in <br/>equity markets. The 200-day MA acts as a major long-term trend indicator: stocks trading above <br/>it are considered in uptrends, below it in downtrends. The 50-day MA provides shorter-term <br/>trend context. When the 50-day crosses above the 200-day, it's called a 'golden cross' - <br/>historically bullish. The opposite (50-day crossing below 200-day) is called a 'death cross' - <br/>historically bearish. <br/>RSI and Momentum <br/>The Relative Strength Index (RSI) measures the speed and magnitude of price changes on a <br/>scale of 0 to 100. An RSI above 70 suggests the stock may be overbought and due for a <br/>pullback. Below 30 suggests oversold conditions and potential for a bounce. RSI divergence - <br/>when price makes a new high but RSI does not - is one of the most reliable signals of a trend <br/>weakening or reversing.<br/><br/>### Real-World Example<br/>Case Study: A Confirmed Breakout <br/>Stock XYZ has traded in a range between $45 support and $60 resistance for 6 months, testing <br/>both levels multiple times. When it finally closes above $60 on three times its average daily <br/>volume, traders interpret this as a confirmed breakout - the resistance has been overcome with <br/>conviction. The RSI moves from 55 to 72, confirming strong momentum. A price target of $75 is <br/>set by measuring the height of the prior range ($15) and projecting it above the breakout point <br/>($60). Stop-loss is placed at $57 (just below the old resistance, now acting as support). <br/> <br/> <br/>Personal Finance Foundations  |  Page 73<br/><br/>### Quiz Questions<br/># <br/>Question <br/>Answer <br/>1 <br/>What is a support level? <br/>A price level where a stock historically stops <br/>falling and buyers tend to enter the market. <br/>2 <br/>What does an RSI above 70 indicate? <br/>The stock may be overbought and potentially <br/>due for a pullback or consolidation. <br/>3 <br/>What is a breakout? <br/>When a stock price moves above resistance or <br/>below support, often with high volume <br/>confirmation. <br/>4 <br/>What does MACD measure? <br/>Momentum using the relationship between two <br/>exponential moving averages to identify trend <br/>direction and strength. <br/>5 <br/>What is volume confirmation? <br/>A price move accompanied by high trading <br/>volume, which increases the signal's reliability. <br/>6 <br/>What is a golden cross? <br/>When the 50-day moving average crosses above <br/>the 200-day moving average - considered a <br/>bullish signal. <br/>7 <br/>What is RSI divergence? <br/>When price makes a new high or low but RSI <br/>does not - a potential signal that the trend is <br/>weakening. <br/> <br/> <br/>Personal Finance Foundations  |  Page 74",
        "realWorldExamples": "Case Study: A Confirmed Breakout \nStock XYZ has traded in a range between $45 support and $60 resistance for 6 months, testing \nboth levels multiple times. When it finally closes above $60 on three times its average daily \nvolume, traders interpret this as a confirmed breakout - the resistance has been overcome with \nconviction. The RSI moves from 55 to 72, confirming strong momentum. A price target of $75 is \nset by measuring the height of the prior range ($15) and projecting it above the breakout point \n($60). Stop-loss is placed at $57 (just below the old resistance, now acting as support). \n \n \nPersonal Finance Foundations  |  Page 73",
